ewline (by Tewodros Amdeberhan) Since $\int_0^1 x^t\,dx =  rac{1}{1+t}$ for any $t \geq 1$, we also have \[ S = \sum_{k=1}^\infty  \sum_{n=0}^\infty  rac{(-1)^{k-1}}{k} \int_0^1 x^{k2^n}\,dx. \] Again by absolute convergence, we are free to permute the integral and the sums: \[ S = \int_0^1 dx\, \sum_{n=0}^\infty \sum_{k=1}^\infty  rac{(-1)^{k-1}}{k} x^{k2^n} = \int_0^1 dx\, \sum_{n=0}^\infty \log (1 + x^{2^n}). \] Due to the uniqueness of binary expansions of nonnegative integers, we have the identity of formal power series \[  rac{1}{1 - x} = \prod_{n=0}^\infty (1 + x^{2^n}); \] the product converges absolutely for $0 \leq x < 1$. We thus have \[ S = -\int_0^1 \log (1-x)\,dx = \left((1-x) \log (1-x) - (1-x)ight)_0^1 = 1. \] 
